Game Design Document: "FreeLobby" (Working Title)
1. Vision & Core Loop
Pitch: A minimalist, browser-based multiplayer room where introverts can get a quick, safe hit of social interaction with strangers at any hour.

Core Loop: Enter name -> Join/Create Room -> Move around -> Express via emotes/10-char signs -> Mutually agree to reveal names (Vibe Check) -> Leave feeling a bit more connected.

Tone: Calming, safe, low-pressure, ephemeral.

2. Tech Stack & Architecture
Frontend: Phaser (HTML5 Canvas/WebGL). Handles rendering placeholder.png, character movement interpolation, and displaying the UI overlay.

Backend: Node.js with Socket.IO. Handles room management, broadcasting player coordinates, routing chat/emotes, and enforcing server limits.

Hosting: Requires a server capable of running a Node.js daemon (not just static file hosting).

3. Core Mechanics (v1.0)
Rooms: Instanced lobbies. If the active room list hits a server-defined cap, room creation is temporarily disabled to prevent crashes.

Movement: 2D top-down or isometric movement. No collision detection needed for v1.0 beyond the walls of the room to keep the AI-generated code simple.

Emotes: A UI panel with up to 30 simple emotes (smile, wave, coffee cup, sleep, etc.) that spawn as a temporary bubble over the player's head.

Signs: A text input field capped at 10 characters. When submitted, the text hovers over the player for a few seconds.

The Vibe Check (Name Reveal): * Players appear as "Stranger" to each other initially.

Player A clicks Player B and selects "Vibe Check."

Player B receives a gentle, unobtrusive prompt: "A Stranger wants to share names. Comfortable?"

If Yes: Both players see each other's entered names. If No: The prompt fades away silently.

4. UI & UX Elements
Landing Screen: Minimalist. A single text input for "Name" and buttons for "Join Random Room" or "Create Room." No passwords, no logins.

In-Game HUD: * Emote Menu (Grid layout).

Sign Input Box (10-char limit).

Room ID display (for sharing).

"Made with Love" Button: Persistent UI button in the corner. Clicking opens a simple HTML/CSS modal over the canvas with your donation link and a brief thank-you message.

5. Asset List (Minimalist Approach)
To keep your workload light, everything relies on placeholder.png tinted via Phaser's built-in color manipulation, or very simple custom pixel art when you're ready.

Player Sprite: placeholder.png (Tinted different colors for different users).

Room Background: A simple solid color or a tiled placeholder.png.

Emotes: 30 small transparent PNGs (or even just unicode emojis rendered as text over the player to save immense amounts of graphic design time).

UI Elements: Standard HTML/CSS DOM elements overlaid on top of the Phaser canvas (this is much easier for an AI to code than building UI entirely within Phaser).

6. Future Scope (Post v1.0)
Room Customization: Players can place basic furniture (placeholder.png variants).

String Saving: The server serializes the X/Y coordinates of placed furniture into a JSON string, encoded as Base64. The user copies this text string and can paste it later to instantly rebuild their room.

Development Timeline (Estimated: 4–6 Weeks)
Working solo with an AI, the biggest hurdle will be keeping the AI focused on one file at a time. Do not ask the AI to build the whole game at once.

Phase 1: Foundation (Week 1)

Goal: Get a local server running and a character moving on a screen.

Set up the Node.js/Socket.IO server boilerplate.

Set up the Phaser frontend boilerplate.

Achieve basic client-server connection (console logging when a user connects).

Implement basic WASD/Arrow key movement for a single placeholder.png sprite.

Phase 2: Multiplayer & Rooms (Week 2)

Goal: See other people moving.

Implement room creation and joining logic in Node.js.

Broadcast player coordinates to other clients in the same room.

Crucial AI prompt tip: Ask the AI specifically for "client-side interpolation" so the movement doesn't look completely stuttery when data is sent over the network.

Phase 3: Communication (Week 3)

Goal: Emotes and Signs.

Build the HTML/CSS overlay for the Emote grid and Sign input.

Wire the UI to emit Socket.IO events (sendEmote, sendSign).

Listen for those events in Phaser to draw text/icons above the appropriate placeholder.png.

Phase 4: The Vibe Check & Polish (Week 4)

Goal: Name reveals and UI finalization.

Implement the handshake logic for the "Vibe Check."

Build the "Made with Love" donation modal.

Implement server limits (e.g., max 50 rooms, max 10 players per room) to ensure stability.

Test with a few friends.